      "leadSummary": "The cellular scale links chemistry to life. At one extreme, covalent bonds and thermal collisions create local molecular possibilities; at the other, an entire cell grows, partitions its material, and responds to perturbations. In this unit the relevant question is what counts as a cell, which structural properties are universal, and how an imaging or growth measurement can justify a claim about cellular structure. Contrast a bacterial cell coordinating transcription and translation with a nucleated cell that partitions RNA synthesis from cytoplasmic translation. Both can adapt to changes in the environment, but the constraints and regulatory architecture differ. A cell diagram is a model of spatial relationships rather than an exhaustive inventory of every molecule. Readers should practice distinguishing the biological object, the label or measurement, and the investigator's interpretation before deriving conclusions.",
