          "secNumber": "1.8",
          "heading": "Interactive model specifications",
          "content": "### Population doubling\n\n**Question tested by the model:** how strongly does a constant generation interval control a culture's apparent expansion? The curve is N/N0=2^(t/g), where t is elapsed time in hours and g is the doubling interval. Holding t=4 h fixed while shortening g from 2 h to 1 h increases the fold change from four to sixteen. This nonlinear response is why generation time needs an explicit unit and why a small timing error matters when many generations accumulate. If observed counts fall below the curve at later times, carrying capacity, resource exhaustion, lag effects, or death may explain the discrepancy. The model deliberately has no finite nutrient reservoir and should not be extrapolated indefinitely to a real culture.\n\n### Diffraction resolution\n\n**Question tested by the model:** why is magnification different from resolving power? Change wavelength while holding numerical aperture fixed and observe the linear diffraction-scale d\u22480.61\u03bb/NA. At 550 nm and NA=1.25, the estimate is 268.4 nm. Raising NA narrows the predicted minimum separation, whereas adding more display pixels does not change this physical estimate. The curve is intended for introductory lateral light-microscopy reasoning, not electron microscopy or localization-based super-resolution. Real optical resolution also depends on aberration correction, noise, object contrast, emission wavelength, pixel sampling, and a justified resolution definition.",
