          "secNumber": "2.5",
          "heading": "Introduction",
          "content": "The membrane provides a selective boundary without functioning as a perfect wall. Exchange involves lipid chemistry, selective proteins, concentrations, electrical forces, and coordinated trafficking. A student can calculate diffusion down a neutral-solute concentration gradient yet predict ion movement incorrectly when electrical potential is ignored. Similarly, a bright endocytic vesicle does not by itself reveal whether a membrane receptor will be recycled, degraded, or continue signaling. This unit therefore combines equilibrium arguments with kinetics and microscopy. Before using any equation, identify the compartment, sign convention, permeability, and timescale. A useful calculation must be paired with the biological assumption under which it applies; otherwise an apparently precise number becomes an unreliable mechanism claim.",
