          "secNumber": "2.6",
          "heading": "Integrative study: distinguishing membrane flux from membrane inventory",
          "content": "Consider a cell expressing a fluorescent membrane transporter while extracellular substrate is increased through a defined concentration series. At every time point the microscope supplies a bright membrane outline, but a stable membrane signal does not establish constant transport flux. Transporter molecules can redistribute laterally, change conformation, or enter recycling compartments without a large change in total fluorescence. Meanwhile, uptake of substrate can approach saturation even though the driving concentration gradient grows. The aim is to create a study in which carrier activity, membrane physical state, and intracellular accumulation can be separated experimentally.\n\nBegin with the transporter's possible rate law, v=Vmax S/(Km+S), and compare measured initial uptake against the prediction at multiple substrate concentrations. A competing purely passive pathway would often generate an approximately linear relation in a range where permeability and concentration are stable. But the measured curve could flatten because a fluorescent detector saturates, not because a carrier becomes saturated. Resolve this by measuring uptake with an independent method and quantifying a calibration curve for the fluorescence sensor. Include a transport-deficient mutant that is properly localized to the surface; otherwise reduced uptake could be a trafficking phenotype rather than a catalytic defect.\n\nA second experiment photobleaches a region of membrane to examine lateral redistribution of tagged proteins. If fluorescence rapidly recovers, the membrane population may be mobile, but the recovery rate is not the same variable as substrate flux through each protein. Fluorescence recovery can be altered by exchanges among membrane pools or repeated exposure damage. A third measurement perturbs ionic composition and reads voltage. For a monovalent ion, the Nernst potential changes logarithmically with external-to-internal activity, but the net cell voltage is set by more than one ion and by active transport. State the inside-versus-outside sign convention and confirm that a dye reports calibrated voltage rather than only a change in fluorescence.\n\nFinally, follow labeled surface transporter through endosomes and recycling compartments after a brief internalization pulse. A reduction in cell-surface intensity could reflect increased internalization, reduced recycling, or lower total production. Separate these possibilities with total-protein measurements and time-resolved compartment labeling. What initially appeared to be one membrane-property question has become a network of different quantities: lipid mobility, transporter conformation, ionic electrochemical equilibrium, vesicle transfer rates, and substrate accumulation. The exercise is to draw a compartment-and-flux diagram, write one observable for each arrow, and identify at least one control that could distinguish two mechanistic stories producing the same endpoint concentration.",
