          "secNumber": "2.7",
          "heading": "Unit synthesis and interpretation",
          "content": "The membrane module ends with a conservation exercise. Draw an outside compartment, cytosol, endosomal pool, and an active transporter; then write a mass-balance equation in words for each. Inward flux raises cytosolic amount only when net uptake exceeds consumption, export, binding, or sequestration. A Nernst potential indicates one ion's equilibrium balance, not the net state of every solute. A carrier's saturation curve does not reveal whether the transporter stays at the surface or cycles through vesicles. Challenge students to propose two experimental traces with identical final intracellular concentration but distinct time courses. One might reflect rapid influx plus equally rapid efflux; the other slow influx and negligible efflux. The appropriate follow-up is a pulse-chase or initial-rate experiment. This reasoning is more powerful than memorizing a list of transport proteins because it explicitly connects physical gradients, protein mechanisms, compartment amounts, and measurement limitations.",
