          "heading": "Interactive model specifications",
          "content": "### Passive diffusive flux\n\n**Question tested by the model:** how does the sign of the gradient set a passive neutral-solute flux? The plotted proxy is J=0.1(Coutside\u2212Cinside) with concentrations on matched millimolar scales and a fixed normalized permeability. The curve crosses zero where both concentrations match and becomes negative when the internal concentration exceeds the external concentration. A negative flux means net outward movement under this sign convention, not an impossible biological state. Do not transfer this expression to selective ionic channels without including electrical forces; do not confuse flux, which is per unit area and time, with the total solute inventory within the cell.\n\n### Nernst voltage\n\n**Question tested by the model:** what concentration ratio would balance the electrical and chemical contributions for one permeant monovalent positive ion? The equation is E\u224826.7 ln(Coutside/Cinside) mV near 37\u00b0C. With potassium-like values outside=5 mM and inside=140 mM, the simplified equilibrium potential is about \u221289 mV. Equal concentrations make the logarithm zero. Increasing the external concentration while holding the internal one constant makes the potential less negative. This ideal model uses concentrations in place of activities and does not include several simultaneously permeant ions or ATP-driven pumps, so it cannot predict a whole-cell resting voltage by itself.",
