          "secNumber": "3.5",
          "heading": "Introduction",
          "content": "Eukaryotic organelles distribute tasks and prevent some reactions from interfering with others. However, compartments exchange material and information, so their boundaries cannot be understood in isolation. Nuclear import relies on selective transport, mitochondrial respiration uses an electrochemical potential, the endomembrane network sorts cargo, and cytoskeletal filaments position and move structures. A helpful organizing principle is to distinguish localization from flux: knowing where a fluorescent molecule sits is not the same as observing it cross a membrane, travel along a track, or undergo a reaction. Experimental designs should always pair spatial information with time and functional readouts when making causal claims.",
