          "secNumber": "4.5",
          "heading": "Introduction",
          "content": "A signaling pathway maps a transient external or internal cue into a measurable response. Receptor occupation, messenger concentration, kinase state, gene expression, and final cell behavior are different quantities connected by regulated processes. The same occupied receptor fraction may evoke different outputs because of amplification, spare receptors, feedback, and adaptation. This unit uses equilibrium occupancy models, nonlinear transfer functions, and simplified dynamical systems to clarify those distinctions. The equations are deliberately minimal. Their primary purpose is to make a testable prediction and reveal when the observed data require another component, not to reconstruct all molecular states from one curve.",
