          "secNumber": "5.5",
          "heading": "Introduction",
          "content": "Cell reproduction is organized by a changing molecular network rather than a universally fixed timer. DNA must be replicated and appropriately distributed, while the cell's growth and mechanical apparatus adapt to each phase. In mitosis sister chromatids separate; in meiosis homologous chromosomes segregate before sisters, generating the distinctive genetic consequences of sexual reproduction. Checkpoints delay transitions when key requirements are unmet, although imperfect control can produce abnormal outcomes. Time-lapse evidence is particularly important: a single image of many cells does not identify each cell's history. The worked exercises distinguish the fraction of time one cell spends in a phase from the percentage of an asynchronous population found in that phase.",
