          "secNumber": "7.5",
          "heading": "Introduction",
          "content": "Cell stress is a decision-making problem with competing possible outcomes. A cell can repair damage, degrade compromised components, arrest proliferation, adapt its metabolism, enter senescence, or undergo a regulated death program. The same molecular marker may accompany more than one outcome, and a decline in that marker can reflect recovery, selection, or loss of cells. This unit emphasizes experiments with longitudinal tracking, functional recovery, and multiple markers. Mathematical response curves illustrate thresholds and clearance kinetics, but readers must avoid turning an arbitrary normalized signal into a clinical probability or interpreting a simplified half-life as a complete recycling pathway.",
