          "secNumber": "7.7",
          "heading": "Unit synthesis and interpretation",
          "content": "The stress module closes with a branching outcome table. A cell exposed to damage might restore molecular function, permanently exit the cell cycle, activate a death program, or survive with a persistent defect. A temporary caspase signal is not interchangeable with a committed-death fraction; punctate autophagy marker abundance is not equivalent to total degradation; marker disappearance is not repair efficiency. Challenge students to design a pulse-chase experiment in which damaged cargo declines while viable cell number also declines. They must determine whether fluorescence loss reflects degradation inside surviving cells or disappearance of the most damaged cells. Normalizing to remaining cells changes the denominator and may reverse an apparent effect. Longitudinal fate mapping, viability endpoints, and marker-specific controls are required to distinguish these possibilities. The final report should acknowledge mixed cellular responses rather than inventing a single fate for every member of a heterogeneous population.",
