          "secNumber": "8.5",
          "heading": "Introduction",
          "content": "Reliable cell biology requires measurements whose errors and selection effects are understood. Culture changes the biological context; microscopy creates spatially sampled signals; fractionation trades enrichment against recovery; and single-cell methods uncover heterogeneity while introducing their own capture biases. A powerful study often combines complementary methods: spatial imaging locates a candidate process, a biochemical assay tests function, and single-cell analysis asks which cells participate. An assay result is only as strong as its controls and denominator. This unit helps readers plan experiments, calculate interpretable quantities, and document measurements so that another scientist can reproduce both the procedures and the reasoning.",
